{
  "dom_id": "step-e691",
  "name": "Example: Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index",
  "problem": "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty {3}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^{n}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}}{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
